The area formula gives $\frac12 bc\sin A = \frac12 ca\sin B = \frac12 ab\sin C$. Dividing by $\frac12 abc$ yields $\frac{\sin A}{a} = \frac{\sin B}{b} = \frac{\sin C}{c}$, which is the first part.

For the common value, let $O$ be the circumcentre and $M$ the midpoint of $BC$. Since $OB = OC = R$, triangle $OBC$ is isosceles, so $OM \perp BC$ and $OM$ bisects the angle $BOC$; hence $BM = R\sin\angle BOM$. By the central angle theorem:

- if $A < 90^\circ$, then $\angle BOC = 2A$, so $\angle BOM = A$;
- if $A = 90^\circ$, then $BC$ is a diameter and $a = 2R$ directly;
- if $A > 90^\circ$, the central angle on the arc not containing $A$ is $2A > 180^\circ$, so the ordinary angle $\angle BOC$ is $360^\circ - 2A$ and $\angle BOM = 180^\circ - A$.

In the first and third cases $\sin\angle BOM = \sin A$. So in every case $a = 2BM = 2R\sin A$.
